From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] framework: a notification's second line is black

Measured on the device on 2026-10-27, on V89, from a raw framebuffer grab of
the shade. The title of a notification is grey 33 (#de000000, the colour the
system itself calls primary), and everything else in the card is grey 117:
the time after the app name, the body line under the title, and the whole
header of a notification that is collapsed because it is low priority.

Patch 0061 made the app name black by settling the accent. This is the other
half of the same thing: 117 is `secondary_text_material_light` reaching the
card through `notification_secondary_text_color_light`, which is the text
colour of TextAppearance.Material.Notification -- the base style, so it is the
body line as well as the header's own secondary parts.

On this panel the choice is not the one Material is making. A display gives
#8a000000 a contrast of 4.7:1 against a bright white; this panel's white
reflects about 42%, there are sixteen levels in between, and grey 117 is the
eighth of them. Rule 8 of docs/design/eink-ui.md already separates a line from
the one above it by size and weight, which is how a Kindle has always done it,
and rule 1 asks for black text on white. So the second line takes the colour
the title has.

Only notifications, and in three places rather than the two first written
here: the base style and the Info style take it as a text colour
(core/res/res/values/styles_material.xml:469, 485), and
NotificationColorUtil.resolveSecondaryColor() returns it at run time for a
notification that carries a light background of its own
(core/java/com/android/internal/util/NotificationColorUtil.java:548). All
three are notifications, so the change reaches every one of them, which is
what is wanted -- but the count was wrong and is corrected here rather than
left to be rediscovered.

`secondary_text_material_light` itself is left alone -- it is textColorTertiary
and textColorSecondaryInverse for every theme, and `notification_progress_
background_color` reads it too (colors.xml:141), none of which this change is
about. One value, no name added, nothing written above an entry, so the boot
image is as it was.

--- a/core/res/res/values/colors.xml
+++ b/core/res/res/values/colors.xml
@@ -130,7 +130,7 @@
     <drawable name="notification_template_divider_media">#29ffffff</drawable>
     <color name="notification_primary_text_color_light">@color/primary_text_default_material_light</color>
     <color name="notification_primary_text_color_dark">@color/primary_text_default_material_dark</color>
-    <color name="notification_secondary_text_color_light">@color/secondary_text_material_light</color>
+    <color name="notification_secondary_text_color_light">@color/primary_text_default_material_light</color>
     <color name="notification_secondary_text_color_dark">@color/secondary_text_material_dark</color>
 
     <color name="notification_material_background_color">#ffffffff</color>
